 #edb/ref 17592186492912 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492955 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6b42d425-4cde-5eb5-be9e-2e8616d2acbb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492955 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186492906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492969 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186492982 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492969 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8fe411e2-028a-531d-ad89-3fff08a9c9f5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492969 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186492845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492978 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186492969 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492978 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a5424259-87b3-5634-8eb1-23ea6d96d73b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492978 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186364226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492982 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186492932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492982 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a66bdb1b-7763-515f-9f1c-bbad66d3c6ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492982 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186399878 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522901 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522912 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186522901 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186522901 #edb/ref 1071 "For the parametric curve\n\n$$\nx=3t e^{3t},\\quad y=5(t^2+2)e^{-3t},\\quad -\\infty<t<\\infty,\n$$\n\ncalculate $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ as a function of $t$ for $t\\ne - \\frac{1}{3}.$\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx}=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186522901 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1aeeb45a-51fe-5f7f-b6b3-81fb614e69f4" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186522901 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t)=3\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(t\\right)\\right)e^{3t}+3t\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(e^{3t}\\right)\\right),\\quad y'(t)=5\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(t^2+2\\right)\\right)e^{-3t}+5(t^2+2)\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(e^{-3t}\\right)\\right)\n$$\n\n$$\nx'(t)=3e^{3t}+ 3t(3e^{3t}),\\quad y'(t)=5(2t)e^{-3t}+5(t^2+2)(-3e^{-3t})\n$$\n\n$$\nx'(t)=3(1+3t)e^{3t},\\quad y'(t)=5(2t-3(t^2+2))e^{-3t}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{5(2t-3(t^2+2))e^{-3t}}{3(1+3t)e^{3t}}=\\frac{5(2t-3(t^2+2))}{3(1+3t)}e^{-6t}.\n$$\n\nThis derivative is defined for $t\\ne - \\frac{1}{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186522908 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522905 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186522908 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186522908 #edb/ref 1071 "For the parametric curve\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1t}{t+1},\\quad y=\\frac{1t^2}{t+4},\\quad t>0,\n$$\n\ncalculate $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ as a function of $t.$\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx}=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186522908 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "85e9a979-dd16-5177-b9bf-cda7e7a25733" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186522908 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t)=\\frac{1\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(t\\right)\\right)(t+1)-1t\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(t+1\\right)\\right)}{(t+1)^2},\\quad y'(t)=\\frac{1\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(t^2\\right)\\right)(t+4)-1t^2\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(t+4\\right)\\right)}{(t+4)^2}\n$$\n\n$$\nx'(t)=\\frac{1(t+1)-1t(1)}{(t+1)^2},\\quad y'(t)=\\frac{2t(t+4)-1t^2(1)}{(t+4)^2}\n$$\n\n$$\nx'(t)=\\frac{1t+1-1t}{(t+1)^2},\\quad y'(t)=\\frac{2t^2+8t-1t^2}{(t+4)^2}\n$$\n\n$$\nx'(t)=\\frac{1}{(t+1)^2},\\quad y'(t)=\\frac{1t^2+8t}{(t+4)^2}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{\\frac{1t^2+8t}{(t+4)^2}}{\\frac{1}{(t+1)^2}}=\\frac{(1t^2+8t)(t+1)^2}{1(t+4)^2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186522915 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522914 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186522915 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186522915 #edb/ref 1071 "For the parametric curve\n\n$$\nx=3t e^{1t},\\quad y=5(t^2+2)e^{-1t},\\quad -\\infty<t<\\infty,\n$$\n\ncalculate $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ as a function of $t$ for $t\\ne -1.$\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx}=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186522915 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d686c858-3d09-57c7-a18a-662015e75d19" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186522915 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t)=3\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(t\\right)\\right)e^{1t}+3t\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(e^{1t}\\right)\\right),\\quad y'(t)=5\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(t^2+2\\right)\\right)e^{-1t}+5(t^2+2)\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(e^{-1t}\\right)\\right)\n$$\n\n$$\nx'(t)=3e^{1t}+ 3t(1e^{1t}),\\quad y'(t)=5(2t)e^{-1t}+5(t^2+2)(-1e^{-1t})\n$$\n\n$$\nx'(t)=3(1+1t)e^{1t},\\quad y'(t)=5(2t-1(t^2+2))e^{-1t}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{5(2t-1(t^2+2))e^{-1t}}{3(1+1t)e^{1t}}=\\frac{5(2t-1(t^2+2))}{3(1+1t)}e^{-2t}.\n$$\n\nThis derivative is defined for $t\\ne -1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186522916 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522910 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186522916 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186522916 #edb/ref 1071 "For the parametric curve\n\n$$\nx=2t e^{1t},\\quad y=5(t^2+2)e^{-1t},\\quad -\\infty<t<\\infty,\n$$\n\ncalculate $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ as a function of $t$ for $t\\ne -1.$\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx}=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186522916 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d8e50997-ca79-5fb3-921b-0f38c140cd4d" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186522916 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t)=2\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(t\\right)\\right)e^{1t}+2t\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(e^{1t}\\right)\\right),\\quad y'(t)=5\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(t^2+2\\right)\\right)e^{-1t}+5(t^2+2)\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(e^{-1t}\\right)\\right)\n$$\n\n$$\nx'(t)=2e^{1t}+ 2t(1e^{1t}),\\quad y'(t)=5(2t)e^{-1t}+5(t^2+2)(-1e^{-1t})\n$$\n\n$$\nx'(t)=2(1+1t)e^{1t},\\quad y'(t)=5(2t-1(t^2+2))e^{-1t}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{5(2t-1(t^2+2))e^{-1t}}{2(1+1t)e^{1t}}=\\frac{5(2t-1(t^2+2))}{2(1+1t)}e^{-2t}.\n$$\n\nThis derivative is defined for $t\\ne -1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186522919 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522913 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186522919 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186522919 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d0/d0a61578b1ef5383e5e8e9e23c3c6309ede794d6b4f9c47a7ce8103b02f79fe2.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve (shown above)\n\n$$\nx=6\\left(2t{e}^{t}\\right),\\,y=4\\left({t}^{2}+2t\\right),\\,0\\le t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\n find the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186522919 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f38d491a-6dbf-5ea6-9e1d-28ccc7170e0f" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186522919 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=6\\left(2{e}^{t}(t+1)\\right),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=4\\left(2t+2\\right).\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{4\\left(2t+2\\right)}{6\\left(2{e}^{t}(t+1)\\right)}=\\frac{4\\left(1\\right)}{6\\left({e}^{t}\\right)}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{4\\left(1\\right)}{6\\left({e}^{1}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\left(1\\right)}{6\\left(e\\right)}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186522920 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522904 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186522920 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186522920 #edb/ref 1071 "For the parametric curve\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{4t}{t+2},\\quad y=\\frac{4t^2}{t+1},\\quad t>0,\n$$\n\ncalculate $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ as a function of $t.$\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx}=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186522920 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fb2910a3-e9dd-5491-a8ee-7d4f932f22ed" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186522920 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t)=\\frac{4\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(t\\right)\\right)(t+2)-4t\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(t+2\\right)\\right)}{(t+2)^2},\\quad y'(t)=\\frac{4\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(t^2\\right)\\right)(t+1)-4t^2\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(t+1\\right)\\right)}{(t+1)^2}\n$$\n\n$$\nx'(t)=\\frac{4(t+2)-4t(1)}{(t+2)^2},\\quad y'(t)=\\frac{8t(t+1)-4t^2(1)}{(t+1)^2}\n$$\n\n$$\nx'(t)=\\frac{4t+8-4t}{(t+2)^2},\\quad y'(t)=\\frac{8t^2+8t-4t^2}{(t+1)^2}\n$$\n\n$$\nx'(t)=\\frac{8}{(t+2)^2},\\quad y'(t)=\\frac{4t^2+8t}{(t+1)^2}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{\\frac{4t^2+8t}{(t+1)^2}}{\\frac{8}{(t+2)^2}}=\\frac{(4t^2+8t)(t+2)^2}{8(t+1)^2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186522922 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186083240.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186522922 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "48db496b-b8ed-5f24-b555-c212cdd45a54" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523015 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523017 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523015 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523044 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523015 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523049 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523015 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523058 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523015 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523015 #edb/ref 1071 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\cos(2t-\\frac{2\\pi}{3})+8,\\,y=4\\sin(2t-\\frac{2\\pi}{3})-9,\\,\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le t<\\frac{5\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical. Give exact answers in ascending order.\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}, $t=$\n{{field-2}}, $t=$\n{{field-3}}, $t=$\n{{field-4}}." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523015 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "01b49672-000e-56a4-a472-53c03786af3d" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523015 #edb/ref 1074 "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-8\\sin(2t-\\frac{2\\pi}{3}),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=8\\cos(2t-\\frac{2\\pi}{3})\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n8\\cos(2t-\\frac{2\\pi}{3})=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{7\\pi}{12}, \\frac{13\\pi}{12}, \\frac{19\\pi}{12}, \\frac{25\\pi}{12}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{7\\pi}{12}) & =-8\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{13\\pi}{12}) & =8\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{19\\pi}{12}) & =-8\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{25\\pi}{12}) & =8\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\frac{7\\pi}{12}, \\frac{13\\pi}{12}, \\frac{19\\pi}{12}, \\frac{25\\pi}{12}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523022 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523031 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523022 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523022 #edb/ref 1071 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\pi t)-8t,\\,y={e}^{1t},\\,0<t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of the parameter $t$ at which the normal line to the curve is horizontal." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523022 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0c0cf3c7-a877-56af-bcc4-84567b0dae9c" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523022 #edb/ref 1074 "The normal line is horizontal when the tangent line to the curve is vertical. Therefore, we require ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1}{t}-8,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=1{e}^{1t}\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{t}-8=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(\\frac{1}{8})=1{e}^{1\\times (1/8)}=e^{\\frac{1}{8}}\\ne 0,\\,\\,\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed horizontal when $t=\\frac{1}{8}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523025 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523056 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523025 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523059 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523025 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523061 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523025 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523077 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523025 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523025 #edb/ref 1071 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=6\\cos(2t-\\frac{3\\pi}{4})-4,\\,y=3\\sin(2t-\\frac{3\\pi}{4})-7,\\,\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le t<\\frac{5\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical. Give exact answers in ascending order.\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}, $t=$\n{{field-2}}, $t=$\n{{field-3}}, $t=$\n{{field-4}}." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523025 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "146bf2ed-a4b6-50b6-a118-3016f8e80fe9" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523025 #edb/ref 1074 "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-12\\sin(2t-\\frac{3\\pi}{4}),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6\\cos(2t-\\frac{3\\pi}{4})\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n6\\cos(2t-\\frac{3\\pi}{4})=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{5\\pi}{8}, \\frac{9\\pi}{8}, \\frac{13\\pi}{8}, \\frac{17\\pi}{8}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{5\\pi}{8}) & =-12\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{9\\pi}{8}) & =12\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{13\\pi}{8}) & =-12\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{17\\pi}{8}) & =12\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\frac{5\\pi}{8}, \\frac{9\\pi}{8}, \\frac{13\\pi}{8}, \\frac{17\\pi}{8}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523026 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523039 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523026 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523026 #edb/ref 1071 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=7(t-4),\\,y=3{t}^{3}-18t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523026 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1b45aef8-5aba-59a4-a0c0-7ae74ccf1ed7" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523026 #edb/ref 1074 "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=7,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=9{t}^{2}-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n9({t}^{2}-2)=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\pm \\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(-\\sqrt{2}) & =7\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\sqrt{2}) & =7\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\pm \\sqrt{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523029 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523055 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523029 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523063 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523029 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523071 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523029 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523084 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523029 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523029 #edb/ref 1071 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\cos(2t-\\frac{\\pi}{3})+3,\\,y=3\\sin(2t-\\frac{\\pi}{3})+4,\\,2\\pi\\le t<4\\pi.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical. Give exact answers in ascending order.\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}, $t=$\n{{field-2}}, $t=$\n{{field-3}}, $t=$\n{{field-4}}." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523029 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "236ca542-1844-556b-915c-8a48dc544a06" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523029 #edb/ref 1074 "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-8\\sin(2t-\\frac{\\pi}{3}),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6\\cos(2t-\\frac{\\pi}{3})\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n6\\cos(2t-\\frac{\\pi}{3})=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{29\\pi}{12}, \\frac{35\\pi}{12}, \\frac{41\\pi}{12}, \\frac{47\\pi}{12}.\n$$\n\nNow, 